+The schema is compiled once at registration, so a schema that is not valid JSON Schema fails at startup. Validation covers `required`, declared property types and `enum` -- it does not reject undeclared properties, coerce types, or fill in defaults, and there is no opt-out.
+
 ### Writing the executor
 
 A `ToolExecutor` is an async function that takes the tool input and returns either a string or a `ToolResponse`:
@@ -132,9 +134,9 @@ type ToolExecutor = (input: Record<string, unknown>, context?: ToolContext) => P
 
 The optional `context` provides `userId`, `sessionId`, `workspaceId`, and `authHeaders` for tools that need to make authenticated API calls.
 
+Input reaching an executor has already passed the declared schema: a required field is present, and every present field has its declared type. Unknown keys may still be present, because validation does not set `additionalProperties: false` -- forward or ignore them as you choose. When a call fails the schema, the registry never invokes the executor; it returns a rejection to the model as a tool result instead of throwing, so the model can correct the call on its next round. See [tool-input-validation](../specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md) for the full contract.
+
 - Return `JSON.stringify(result)` for most tools -- the result gets added to the conversation as a tool result message
-- Cast input fields from `unknown` to their expected types
-- Handle missing or unknown input gracefully (return a reasonable default or error message)
 
 For tools that need to send messages directly to the client or suppress the AI's echo, return a `ToolResponse` object instead of a plain string. See [tool-responses.md](../specs/hal-engine-tool-responses/spec.md) for a full walkthrough.
 
